-- One row per customer per consent.
--
-- Generated by `pnpm --filter @vyloop/db db:generate` and reviewed by hand, like
-- `0013_sour_apocalypse.sql` and `0014_branch_geolocation.sql`.
--
-- ## What this closes
--
-- The `consents` table was read by two surfaces and written by none. It is the
-- model the specifications describe — a consent type, a granted flag, and the
-- timestamps that make a withdrawal auditable — and it was always empty.
--
-- A table that is written by more than one path cannot rely on every writer
-- remembering to check whether a row already exists. "One row per customer per
-- consent type" has to be a constraint, not a convention: with two rows for one
-- consent, a gate that asks "has this customer agreed" gets two answers for
-- one question, and which one it reads is an accident of ordering.
--
-- ## Why the index covers the whole triple
--
-- It is unique over `(business_id, customer_id, consent_type)` and not over
-- `(customer_id, consent_type)`. The same person is a different customer at a
-- different merchant, and a consent given to one of them says nothing about
-- the other.
--
-- It is not partial either. A withdrawn consent keeps its row — that is what
-- makes the withdrawal auditable — and a partial index would therefore let a
-- second row for the same type be written the moment the first was withdrawn.
--
-- ## Why an index and not a table constraint
--
-- Because that is how this repository registers a uniqueness rule, and the
-- registry is derived rather than declared: `packages/db/src/indexes.ts` reads
-- `getTableConfig(table).indexes`, and a `unique()` constraint is not in that
-- list. Declaring this one as a constraint would have left it invisible to the
-- registry, and `migration-sql.test.ts` — which asserts that the
-- `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX` statements in the migration history are exactly the set
-- the registry reports — would have failed.
--
-- `uq_customers_business_member_code` is the precedent.
--
-- ## What this migration does NOT do
--
-- **It does not backfill.** There is nothing to backfill: the table has no rows,
-- and the jsonb flag the gates used to read has no rows either, in production
-- and in the local database alike. A backfill of zero rows is not a migration.
--
-- **It does not enable row-level security.** `consents` is listed in
-- `BUSINESS_ID_TABLES`, so it receives a policy when the separate, reviewed RLS
-- rollout runs. That rollout is this table's isolation story and it must not
-- arrive by accident in an index.
--
-- ## Not applied here
--
-- `drizzle-kit migrate` against production is forbidden in this repository: the
-- ledger has drift and running the migrator would apply migrations twice. A
-- person applies this by hand, after review. This file is left on disk, unrun.
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX "uq_consents_customer_type" ON "consents" USING btree ("business_id","customer_id","consent_type");
